Asahi M4 driver regression tests
===============================

From a kernel checkout, `make` builds/installs no generated harnesses and
requires no Rust compiler. `make cpu`, `make run_tests` and the kselftest entry
point `./run.sh` run CPU-only regressions. They never open a render node or submit GPU work. They need Python 3,
a host Rust compiler (edition 2021) and a host C compiler. RUSTC and CC may be
set to compiler command lines; no private checkout or kernel build output is
needed. Missing compilers produce a kselftest skip (exit 4).

Results and generated host harnesses go into a fresh temporary directory;
`ASAHI_M4_OUTPUT=/path/to/results make cpu` selects a retained output directory.
Installed tests need ASAHI_M4_KERNEL=/path/to/kernel-source. The in-tree suite
finds its own checkout. Each test compiles actual production Rust modules or
method bodies with CPU-only memory, allocation, MMIO and kernel API stand-ins.
These tests do not qualify hardware MMIO, cache effects, firmware lifetime,
performance, desktop operation or graphics API conformance.

The fixtures directory preserves independent C-generated firmware bytes and
VM oracle results from kernel commit 5e86481d356cdfa05d703b4b97f1cb56aa8e2819.
manifest.json pins the original C helper hashes, compressed/uncompressed byte
hashes, historical generator inputs and UAPI bindings. The reference commits
are provenance from the pre-removal development history, not dependencies on
the proposed commit ancestry. The historical *.input.c files reference helpers
that are not shipped here; they do not form a complete reproducible generator.
Keep the original source snapshots when reviewing fixture replacement. The C helpers
were checked with address/undefined-behavior sanitizers during generation.
The removed C GPU driver is not restored or required. *.input.c are historical
fixture inputs only; no test compiles them. Fixtures must never be regenerated
from the Rust implementation being tested. Protocol changes require reviewing
and documenting an independent reference before replacing these fixtures.

The byte fixtures cover initdata, loader spans and bounds, compute work,
allocator images, TA/FS work and sequences, timestamps, submit records,
queue/context/notifier records and TTBAT generation words. Comparisons include
3,736,988 encoder bytes and 598 bounds/loader cases. Frozen Rust UAPI bindings
are compared against the actual kernel header by compiling complete C and Rust
definitions and measuring sizes, alignments, member offsets and constants.
Comments and documentation can change without invalidating that ABI check.
The current global-parameters reply has a documented additive capacity tail.
The frozen 600-byte prefix is compared unchanged; independent C assertions
require the full reply to be exactly 616 bytes, with two eight-byte fields at
600 and 608. Older Mesa headers can still match the shared prefix. Firmware
encoder golden data and its fixture manifest are never regenerated.
No Mesa headers are vendored. Set ASAHI_M4_MESA=/path/to/mesa, or run
`test-uapi.py --mesa /path/to/mesa`, for a compatibility check against the real
userspace source; a default host run reports that Mesa comparison was omitted.

The thermal suite compiles the shared production C policy/sensor headers
against deterministic regression traces: 571,828 policy rows, 75,414 sensor
cases, and independent rounding/boundary checks. Rust tests exercise only the
GPU scheduling and request-selection helpers; sensor decoding and thermal
policy are owned by PMP rather than duplicated in the GPU. The
PPL tests exercise the actual shared RAII handshake core with competing CPU
owners, firmware-yield/timeout/error paths and guard-drop ordering; separate
VM and retention harnesses check publication and memory ownership with mock
hardware. Resource/recovery/frontend tests cover real capacity admission,
dirty-span clearing, abort receipts, command validation and fence completion.

GPU execution is opt-in and requires a running M4 Rust Asahi kernel and the
paired native Mesa installation:

  make hardware MESA=/path/to/installed/mesa RENDER_NODE=/dev/dri/renderD128 ROUNDS=32

This builds native-gl46.c, verifies asahi-g16 owns the render node, selects the
requested Mesa libraries, clears software/translation/API-version overrides,
and runs native OpenGL 4.6 triangle readback, compute and geometry-feedback
checks. Results include kernel/boot identity and Mesa/binary hashes. Ordinary
make and kselftest targets do not invoke this hardware target. A passing host
suite is not permission to skip the reboot and hardware regression after a
kernel change.
